  <section id="the-four-records">
    <h2><span class="num">2.1</span>The four records</h2>
    <p class="first">
      The planned product keeps four kinds of record. Paragraphs of law and calendar deadlines get IDs
      that stay the same every time the tool runs, and rules have codes such as OBL-01. So any date on
      the calendar can be traced back to the paragraph of law it came from, and to the human specialist
      who approved it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Tracking whether a report has been sent is out of scope for now. Because each deadline keeps the
      same ID, a future record of what was sent can point to it.
    </p>
    <Records />
  </section>

  <section id="who-may-do-what">
    <h2><span class="num">2.2</span>Who may do what</h2>
    <p>
      Working with AI, I drafted an approval map: every action in the planned product, and whether it
      can happen on its own, needs a human specialist’s approval, or must never happen. The table below
      simplifies it. The most important cells say <strong>Never</strong>: no AI agent can ever approve,
      reject or publish anything, and no AI can set a date.
    </p>
    <PermissionsTable />
  </section>

  <section id="one-decision-at-a-time">
    <h2><span class="num">2.3</span>One decision at a time</h2>
    <p>
      A human specialist approves, edits or rejects each rule on its own. There is no “approve all”
      button, and nothing is ticked in advance. Separate clicks encourage careful review, but they can’t
      prove it happened.
    </p>
    <!-- Next PR: the simulated checkpoint 1 demo, its audit log, and the design question card -->
  </section>

  <section id="when-not-sure-is-right">
    <h2><span class="num">2.4</span>When “not sure” is right</h2>
    <p>
      Sometimes the honest answer is “not sure”. The fictional fund is offered to investors in Spain,
      but nobody has confirmed it is registered with the Spanish regulator. The Spanish report is only
      required for registered funds, so the right answer is “not sure”, and a human specialist decides.
      An AI that answers “yes, it applies” sounds reasonable but is wrong.
    </p>
    <!-- Later PR: the OBL-04 verdict card and the answer-key code tabs -->
  </section>

  <section id="dates-come-from-code">
    <h2><span class="num">2.5</span>Dates come from code</h2>
    <p>
      Month ends, leap years and weekends are easy to get wrong, so no AI will set a date. Plain code
      will take a starting date, add the time allowed, then apply that report’s own weekend rule, set by
      a human specialist. Some deadlines move to the next working day if they land on a weekend or
      public holiday. Tax deadlines stay where they fall. If nobody has confirmed the rule, the earlier
      date is kept and marked, because filing early is safe.
    </p>
    <!-- Later PR: the simulated date calculator -->
  </section>

